    <h2>Compare yearly costs, not sticker prices</h2>
    <p>Every tool in this section turns a large one-time purchase into a cost per year, so it can be compared fairly with keeping what you have. A $1,600 refrigerator that lasts 11 years and a $450 repair that buys 3 more years look very different once both are spread over the years they deliver.</p>

    <h3>Repair or replace</h3>
    <p>A common rule of thumb says to replace an appliance when a repair costs more than half the price of a new one, especially past the midpoint of its expected life. The <a href="../../calculators/repair-or-replace-appliance.html">repair or replace calculator</a> goes further by comparing the yearly cost of each option, including the energy savings of a newer model and the chance the repair doesn't last. In its default example, the most worth paying to fix an 8-year-old refrigerator is about $346, so a $450 quote points to replacing it.</p>

    <h3>Heat pump vs. furnace</h3>
    <p>A heat pump moves heat rather than making it, so it can deliver 2 to 3 units of heat for each unit of electricity in mild weather, with efficiency falling as it gets colder. Whether it costs less to run than a furnace depends mainly on the price of electricity relative to your fuel and on your climate. The <a href="../../calculators/heat-pump-vs-furnace.html">heat pump vs. furnace calculator</a> compares running cost, payback, and emissions; at its default rates in a moderate climate, a new gas furnace is cheaper to run, while replacing propane, oil, or electric resistance heat usually favors the heat pump. A heat pump also replaces the air conditioner, which belongs in the comparison if yours is due for replacement.</p>

    <h3>Solar panels</h3>
    <p>Solar payback depends on 3 numbers: your installed cost, how much sun your location gets, and what each kilowatt-hour you avoid buying is worth, which depends on your electricity rate and how your utility credits power sent back to the grid. The <a href="../../calculators/solar-payback.html">solar payback calculator</a> uses state-average rates and production, and checks whether waiting for cheaper panels would pay. The federal residential clean energy credit ended after 2025, so current payback figures are based on the full installed cost unless state or utility incentives apply.</p>

    <h2>Get better inputs</h2>
    <p>Your utility bills show your actual rates and yearly usage; use 12 months of bills rather than one. For heating, the fuel delivered over a winter is more reliable than an estimate. For repairs and installations, a written quote beats an online average every time.</p>
  </div>
